# The installation this machine becomes. Read by install-machine.sh at a machine's birth, and by
# regenerate-install-branch.sh whenever an installation is brought to the release its own map is
# pinned to.
#
# key=value and not JSON: a shell reads it with one `.` and needs no parser, and a
# comment is the only place a value can say what it is for.
#
# Single quotes, nothing is expanded. A value carrying a single quote is refused
# before the machine is touched. A list is
# comma-separated; the programs checkout says which values are lists.

# SECRETS. Each carries `secret: true` in the program that declares it. All but the
# first are credentials a third party issued; the first raises commands to root.

ELEVATION_PASSWORD=''
# THE TWO TOKENS OF THE PLATFORM REPOSITORY, a pair like the two it has had from the start: the WRITER
# commits the cluster maps and the release pins, the READER only syncs. While PLATFORM_REPO names the
# repository on github.com they are GitHub fine-grained tokens, Contents: Read and Write for the writer
# and Contents: Read for the reader. Once PLATFORM_REPO names the same repository as
# FORGEJO_PLATFORM_REPOSITORY the tree is served from this installation's Forgejo, and both hold a
# Forgejo access token for that one repository instead. The reader is then REQUIRED, because the
# repository is private there and the clone, the pin and the probe for the install branch all present
# it.
PLATFORM_REPO_WRITE_PAT=''
PLATFORM_REPO_READ_PAT=''
REGISTRY_DOCKERHUB_TOKEN=''
BUILD_PLATFORM_REPO_WRITE_PAT=''
BUILD_MANAGER_REPO_WRITE_PAT=''

# THE PRIVATE KEY OF THE PLATFORM'S OWN GITHUB APP, installed with the owner of the deploy repository and
# of every tenant repository: the identity the deploy repository is read and written with everywhere (the
# reconciler of every cluster, the Manager, the release pipeline's bump), and the identity the
# Manager creates a tenant's own apps repository under and mints the tokens that build and deploy
# it with. Its installation grants repository administration (create), contents (write),
# workflows (write) and webhooks (write). Not a person's token: a repository made for a customer
# is made by the platform, under its own name. The App's two ids and its owner stand among the
# settings below, because they are not secrets; the Manager reads all four as GITHUB_APP_ID,
# GITHUB_APP_INSTALLATION_ID, GITHUB_APP_PRIVATE_KEY and GITHUB_APP_OWNER out of
# secret/<stage>/app/github-app.
# Required, all four: an installation without the App does not exist.
#
# THE KEY IS A PEM FILE, AND THIS FILE HOLDS ONE LINE PER VALUE. Write it as one line with `\n`
# in place of each line break. The quotes here expand nothing and the seed copies the value as
# it stands, so the two characters reach the Manager, which turns them back.
GITHUB_APP_PRIVATE_KEY=''

STORAGE_BOX_PASSWORD=''
# WHAT A MASTER'S BACKUP IS SEALED WITH before it leaves the machine (master-backup), and opened
# with when it comes back (master-restore): a passphrase, symmetric, gpg. The archive carries Vault's
# data AND its unseal keys, the Manager's database and the IdP's accounts — every credential of the
# installation in one place — so the storage box holds it sealed and this value never travels with
# it. Lose this and the backups are noise; it belongs in the same place the other lines here do.
BACKUP_PASSPHRASE=''

# A Cloudflare token for DNS ONLY: Zone:DNS:Edit and Zone:Zone:Read, scoped to BOTH zones of
# this installation, the map's platformDomain and unitApex, and with no account-level
# permission. The DNS-01 solver of platform-acme answers every certificate in either zone with
# this token, so a zone missing from it leaves that zone's certificates unrenewed until they
# expire. A token for another
# Cloudflare product fits this line and cannot read a zone, and nothing notices until the
# first unit's record (hostyour-deploy#28).
CLOUDFLARE_DNS_API_TOKEN=''
# THE ORIGIN PULL CERTIFICATE, which Cloudflare presents to the origins of a proxied host, and the
# CA the origins verify it by. create-origin-pull-certificate.sh writes all three, each PEM on one
# line with `\n` for each line break, and a second run of it is a rotation. The CA is public: the
# seed carries it into secret/<stage>/app/cloudflare-origin-pull, where every cluster's TLSOption
# cloudflare-origin-pull reads it. The certificate and its key go to Cloudflare alone, through
# apply-cloudflare-zone-security.sh, and never leave this file otherwise.
CLOUDFLARE_ORIGIN_PULL_CA=''
CLOUDFLARE_ORIGIN_PULL_CERTIFICATE=''
CLOUDFLARE_ORIGIN_PULL_PRIVATE_KEY=''
# A THIRD Cloudflare token, for apply-cloudflare-zone-security.sh alone: Zone Read, Zone Settings
# Write, SSL and Certificates Write and Zone WAF Write, on the zones platformDomain and unitApex.
# It stays in this file and never enters the secret store, because no cluster changes a zone's
# security; NOT the DNS token above, which every cluster holds.
CLOUDFLARE_ZONE_SECURITY_API_TOKEN=''
# A SECOND Cloudflare token, for object storage, and a MANAGING one. TWO permissions on
# the account named below: Workers R2 Storage Write makes a bucket, Account API Tokens
# Write mints and withdraws the per-tenant keys — an R2 access key IS an account API
# token, so with only the first the bucket is made and the key is refused. The platform
# makes each tenant's bucket with it and mints a key reaching that one bucket, so no
# tenant ever holds this token. NOT the zone token above, and NOT the Access Key ID /
# Secret Access Key pair the dashboard shows beside it: those are what the platform
# mints per tenant, and one pair here would reach every tenant's objects.
CLOUDFLARE_R2_API_TOKEN=''

# SETTINGS. What the five programs declare. Each is refused by name if missing.


OPERATOR_USER=''
PLATFORM_REPO=''
# the branch deploy-host CLONES the platform tree on — 'master' for a machine born as a master
PLATFORM_BRANCH=''
# THE STATE THIS MACHINE IS BORN ON, as a release tag such as 0.7.9-stable-20260903202414.
# deploy-branch merges it into the branch it cuts and writes it as the `release:` pin of this
# cluster's map, which is what every later regeneration reads. It is NOT PLATFORM_BRANCH above:
# that one names what is cloned, this one names the state the install branch is put on. A tag has
# to stand on the platform repository already, and a FIRST machine has no install branch for one to
# be cut against: release-platform.sh with a version and a channel and NO fqdn mints the tag, pushes
# it and pins nothing, which is the form that answers this key.
PLATFORM_REF=''
OPERATOR_PUBLIC_KEY=''
FQDN=''
# THE DOOR, WHERE IT IS NOT THE NAME. FQDN above is the installation's identity: the install branch,
# every endpoint under it, every certificate. This is the address a session to the MACHINE is opened
# at — install-machine, regenerate-install-branch, master-backup and master-restore all knock here.
# Empty means the identity is the door, which is every installation whose name points at its one
# machine. It is stated where the name points elsewhere: a master reached as `master.<apex>` that is
# one of two machines, `master1.<apex>` and `master2.<apex>`, of which the name follows the live one
# — then a standby is installed and kept through its own name while the identity still points at
# the other. A CNAME, never a second identity: nothing on the machine reads this but the launchers.
MACHINE_HOST=''
LOGIN_SERVER=''
# one of [dev, test, prod]
STAGE=''
# every part this machine carries — one of [master, slave, master+slave]
ROLE=''
# THE CLUSTER THAT KEEPS THE BOOKS — the cluster maps and the registrations this installation
# knows about itself. Empty where this cluster keeps them itself; the other cluster's domain
# where it does not, which is what a slave states.
BOOKS_FQDN=''
BUILD_PLANE_FQDN=''
UNIT_APEX=''
PLATFORM_DOMAIN=''
# a list: separate entries with a comma
ALERT_RECIPIENTS=''
# owner/name of THIS INSTALLATION'S OWN repository of tenant charts — private, reached with the
# platform's GitHub App. Not the repository the programs come from; that one is PROGRAMS_REPO.
DEPLOY_REPO=''
# owner/name of the public repository the deployment programs are read from. The platform's
# own, the same for every installation, and cloned without a credential.
PROGRAMS_REPO='simetrixch/hostyour-deploy'
LAN_HOST=''
COMMITTER_NAME=''
COMMITTER_EMAIL=''
# THE STORAGE BOX master-backup writes to and master-restore reads from, with STORAGE_BOX_PASSWORD above.
STORAGE_BOX_HOST=''
STORAGE_BOX_USER=''
# may stay as the declared default platform-pull
REGISTRY_PULL_USER=''
# may stay as the declared default platform-push
REGISTRY_PUSH_USER=''
# THE SSH PORT FORGEJO ANNOUNCES in its clone address, ssh://git@forgejo.<fqdn>:<port>/, and the
# port the deployment scans Forgejo's SSH host key on, at FORGEJO_HOST below. The master listens on
# 2222, and the router forwards the public port to that one. State the public port, for example 23
# where the router forwards 23: the scan runs from the machine that runs the program, through that
# address, and where it answers nothing the program refuses naming the address and stores no host
# key for the release bump. Empty means the declared default 2222, which answers only where the
# router forwards 2222 itself.
FORGEJO_SSH_PORT=''
# THE HOST THIS INSTALLATION'S FORGEJO IS SERVED FROM, without a scheme or a path, spelled the way
# the Manager spells it (its FORGEJO_HOST), for example forgejo.master.example.org on the master
# master.example.org. A slave states its master's, because an installation has one Forgejo. The
# platform repository is cloned, its pin is read and the root Application is pointed at this host
# once the tree is served from Forgejo, and its SSH host key is scanned here.
FORGEJO_HOST=''
# REQUIRED DESTINATIONS on the installation's Forgejo, even while clone URLs still name GitHub: the
# host above and the three lines below. PLATFORM_REPO stays the GitHub name until the tree has
# moved; the day it names the same repository as FORGEJO_PLATFORM_REPOSITORY, the tree is served
# from this Forgejo. Each repository is owner/repository. The comma-separated organisations must
# include both owners; install-machine.sh and regenerate-install-branch.sh refuse a config where
# one of the four is missing.
FORGEJO_PLATFORM_REPOSITORY=''
FORGEJO_DEPLOY_REPOSITORY=''
FORGEJO_ORGANISATIONS=''
# THE OWNER'S ACME BLOCK: the one ACME account of the installation, at Google Trust Services, the
# one authority this platform issues from. Each stays one line in the form KEY='value', which is the
# form the drivers read. ACME_EMAIL is the mailbox the authority writes to before a certificate
# expires, and ACME_SERVER its directory, the only one deploy-cluster admits. ACME_CREDENTIALS_JSON is
# the External Account Binding Google issued, as one line of JSON with "keyId" and "b64MacKey"; the
# drivers answer acme_eab_key_id and acme_eab_mac_key from it, and refuse a line without both by this
# key's name, never with its value. One binding admits one account, once, within seven days of its
# creation. An ACME_PROVIDER line, where a config carries one, is read by nothing: there is one
# provider.
# A FIRST INSTALL LEAVES ACME_CREDENTIALS_JSON EMPTY, and install-machine refuses it stated, because
# an installation is born on platform-local; it is stated afterwards with CLUSTER_ISSUER='platform-acme',
# the install branch regenerated and the Manager's cluster-redeploy of the machine run. ACME_EMAIL and
# ACME_SERVER stay stated from the first install on: deploy-cluster asks both and admits only GTS.
ACME_EMAIL=''
ACME_SERVER='https://dv.acme-v02.api.pki.goog/directory'
ACME_CREDENTIALS_JSON=''
# THE TIME SERVERS THIS INSTALLATION'S MACHINES ASK, comma-separated on one line, written into
# chrony's sources by deploy-host, which then refuses while the clock stays out of step. REQUIRED:
# deploy-host empties the pools the distribution names, so a machine with no answer here would ask
# no time server at all, and the program refuses before its first step. Name the provider's where
# the provider answers no other (Hetzner answers ntp1.hetzner.de, ntp2.hetzner.de, ntp3.hetzner.de
# and drops the rest).
TIME_SOURCES=''
# THE HOST LABEL OF THIS INSTALLATION'S MAIL SERVICE, if it runs one: one DNS label, the `host` its
# manifest declares (for this product's consumer digita-post that is `post`), no scheme and no zone.
# Every unit composes the address itself, as https:// followed by that label and the unit's own
# stage zone — `post.digitacloud.app` at prod, `post.dev.digitacloud.app` at dev — so a test unit
# reaches the test mail service and a prod unit the prod one. A tenant's auth and report members
# require it and stop their render without it, so leave it empty ONLY on an installation that runs
# no mail service: an empty value is left out of the map, and the members then say what they lack
# by name (deploy-branch's answer mail_host).
MAIL_HOST=''
# THE ACCOUNT THE TENANT BUCKETS ARE MADE IN, as the 32-character id the Cloudflare dashboard
# shows. Not a secret: it stands in every bucket's public endpoint and travels in the cluster map,
# where the tenant charts read it to compose one. Answer it together with the token above or
# neither: a tenant creation on an installation missing one of them is refused by name, which is
# where it has to be noticed.
CLOUDFLARE_R2_ACCOUNT_ID=''
# THE TWO IDS OF THE PLATFORM'S GITHUB APP AND ITS OWNER, whose private key stands among the secrets
# above: the App's own id, the id of its installation in the customer's organisation — the number
# in the address of that installation's settings page, because one App may be installed in
# many organisations — and the login of the account that installation belongs to. None is a secret:
# the App id is the issuer every token the App mints names, and it is also written into this
# cluster's manager values as the switch that renders the App's secret at all, so an installation
# that answers no App renders nothing for one. The owner is the prefix of the repositories the
# installation reaches: the credential Argo CD is handed for the App carries it, whichever forge
# serves the platform repository. create-github-app.sh writes it from the installation GitHub
# names for the App, so it is never worked out from an address. Answer these three together with
# the key above, or none of the four.
GITHUB_APP_ID=''
GITHUB_APP_INSTALLATION_ID=''
GITHUB_APP_OWNER=''
# THE JURISDICTION, AND IT IS DELIBERATELY NOT A LINE OF THIS FILE. Cloudflare hands nobody one:
# `default` is what an account that never chose otherwise has, and the program declares that as the
# answer's default, so an installation that says nothing here is already right.
#
# A LINE IN THIS FILE IS AN ANSWER, EVEN AN EMPTY ONE — the driver hands over every name this file
# states, whatever it holds — so `CLOUDFLARE_R2_JURISDICTION=''` is refused against the allowed
# three, and a wrong one is worse than a refusal: the jurisdiction is part of a bucket's IDENTITY
# and not a setting on it, so it lands in the endpoint AND in the resource string the tenant's key
# is scoped by, and both then name a bucket that is not there.
#
# Add the line ONLY on an installation whose buckets were deliberately created under `eu` or
# `fedramp`, and then never change it: changed after the first tenant exists it does not move that
# tenant's bucket, it hides it.
# WHICH AUTHORITY ISSUES THIS INSTALLATION'S CERTIFICATES: platform-acme is the public one, whose
# root every browser and container image already carries; platform-local is one the cluster mints
# for itself, which nothing outside the installation trusts. An installation begins on
# platform-local and is flipped to platform-acme once its names resolve.
#
# STATE IT EVEN WHEN IT IS platform-local. An empty value here is not "no answer" the way it is
# above: the programs default it to platform-local, so leaving it empty on an installation that
# stands on platform-acme makes the next regeneration rewrite its issuer and re-issue every
# certificate from the cluster's own root - which the public certificates it already holds were
# rate-limited to obtain.
CLUSTER_ISSUER='platform-local'
# one of [alpha, beta, stable]
CHANNEL=''
# may stay as the declared default http://127.0.0.1:8484
MANAGER_URL=''
